<table cellpadding="3" cellspacing="0" border="0" width="100%" align="center" class="tborder" style="border-bottom: 0;">
	<tr class="titlebg">
		<td valign="middle" align="left" width="15%" style="padding-left: 6px;">
			<img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/topic/normal_post.gif" alt="" />
			Author
		</td>
		<td valign="middle" align="left" width="85%" style="padding-left: 6px;">
			Topic: Chapter 4 &nbsp;(Read 132 times)
		</td>
	</tr>
</table>
<table cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" width="100%" class="bordercolor">
	<tr><td style="padding: 1px 1px 0 1px;">
		<table cellpadding="3" cellspacing="0" border="0" width="100%">
			<tr><td class="windowbg">
				<table width="100%" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0">
					<tr>
						<td valign="top" width="15%" rowspan="2">
							<b><a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?action=profile;u=81" title="View the profile of Phoebie">Phoebie</a></b><br />
							<span class="smalltext">
								Orc Pawn<br />
								<img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/star.gif" alt="*" border="0" /><br />
								Posts: 15<br />
								<br />
								<img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/YaBBImages/avatars/blank.gif" alt="" border="0" /><br />
								I'm a llama!<br />
								<br />
								
								
								
								<br />
								<a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?action=profile;u=81"><img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/icons/profile_sm.gif" alt="View Profile" title="View Profile" border="0" /></a>
								<a href="mailto:brneydmzo@bellsouth.net"><img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/email_sm.gif" alt="Email" title="Email" border="0" /></a>
							</span>
						</td>
						<td valign="top" width="85%" height="100%">
							<table width="100%" border="0"><tr>
								<td align="left" valign="middle"><a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?topic=118.msg373#msg373"><img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/post/xx.gif" alt="" border="0" /></a></td>
								<td align="left" valign="middle">
									<b><a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?topic=118.msg373#msg373">Chapter 4</a></b><br />
									<span class="smalltext">&#171; <b> on:</b> April 10, 2003, 02:01:55 pm &#187;</span></td>
								<td align="right" valign="bottom" height="20" nowrap="nowrap" style="font-size: smaller;">
								</td>
							</tr></table>
							<hr width="100%" size="1" class="hrcolor" />
							The water was cold today, colder than usual, and as the strapping barbarian tried vainly to bait the small hook at the end of his bamboo fishing pole, his large fingers trembled with cold and he slipped, jabbing the sharp hook into his right thumb.<br /><br />&quot;Aiyeeee! DAMMIT!&quot; howled the now grimacing barbarian, and in frustration and anger, he kicked the tacklebox next to him, sending lures and bobbers and fishing wire careening through the air, scattering them across the snow that lined the banks of the frigid lake.<br /><br />Kaidog was a large barbarian, hair and beard and moustache as black as night, arms scarred and scraped from encounters with tundra wolves and polar bears, and the distinguishing scar across his left cheek, left courtesy of Lady Vox on that day long ago when he had first laid eyes on her as his clan raided her lair, in search of a lost girl they were sure had been kidnapped<br />by a goblin and brought to the ice dragon as a token of their loyalties to her. &nbsp;Unfortunately, the girl was never found, but Lady Vox was more than happy to show them the reasons legends were written about her. &nbsp;That day alone, 27 of Kaidog's clan members fell to her icy wrath, and he had barely managed to escape, by pure luck and sheer determination. &nbsp;He remembered with a shudder how he had collapsed at the door of the Halas Tribunal, bleeding and out of breath, a gash so deep across his cheek that the queasy shaman initiate who had grudgingly bandaged his wounds nearly lost her lunch as she doctored him. &nbsp;That shaman, his friend Karadana, had long since left the igloos of Everfrost in search of adventure in the melted lands far south from where she belonged. &nbsp;He remembered with a smile how they used to hunt together as young children and teenagers, and with a shooting wince of regret, he wondered how she was doing, where life had dropped her, and if her heart had replaced him yet, unlike his had yet to replace the memory of her, or probably ever would.<br /><br />He would never admit it, but he had loved her, and far before he knew the power of attraction that age and puberty would bring. &nbsp;As children, they were constantly competing with one another. &nbsp;Young Karadana, the daughter of the head Shaman Karad in Halas had been trained since birth to embrace the shamanistic arts, but her true heart's desire was to be a warrior. &nbsp;Named after her shaman father Karad and her beastlord mother Ana, Karadana would watch the brave warlords around her father's fire late at night when she was a child, their muscular looming frames wrapped in thick animal skins, the frosty tendrils of winter clinging to their beards as they told tale after tale of their rowdy and often perilous adventures. &nbsp;She would stay up until she could not keep her eyes open anymore, face pressed to the curtain separating her room from the common room in the hut, listening intently to the booming voices of the warriors, ready at a moment's notice to feign sleep should her father rise from his stool to check if she was still in bed. &nbsp;<br /><br />One warrior in particular was known to brag a bit too much about his &quot;brave, strapping son&quot;, a pup of a boy named Kaidog who Karadana knew from school, one who made it a habit of driving her insane on a regular basis. Kaidog's father, a warrior known as Tundra Jack in these parts, was a raucous, burly man with a laugh that could be heard echoing through the village, signaling his arrival each evening as he returned from the tundra with sleighs full of animal skins and freshly netted fish.<br /><br />Kaidog loved to torment Karadana. &nbsp;He would watch her pour over the scrolls of magic in the tribunal library every afternoon after day school; shamans had much to learn and little time to learn it in, and Karadana was constantly studying something, practicing some spell, or mixing some smelly potion that she would try to force down his throat. &nbsp;He would never forget the day one of her concoctions caused his skin to burn and blister so excruciatingly that he ran around screaming, tossing himself into the banks of snow between the village huts, cursing her name and providing a healthy portion of entertainment for anyone who happened to be around. &nbsp;Karadana had laughed, eyes large and blue and innocent, but with a twinkle of malevolence that made him realize that perhaps she had done it on purpose.<br /><br />He would always make a clanging entrance into the silent stacks of books where she would study, his armor ridiculously oversized for him at the age of 12, the sword too heavy yet for him to even swing, but knowing how Karadana hated her memorizations, he would derive great pleasure from the look of jealousy and contempt that would cross her pretty face as he would walk into the study, pretending he didn't see her in the corner buried in her spells and incantations, eyes glazing over from too much reading.<br /><br />&quot;You know, it's quite ridiculous the way you look,&quot; Karadana teased. &quot;It is as if a blacksmith threw up on you or something, you are swimming in that armor, you puny string bean. &nbsp;Why not just give up, and become a rogue? You're a sneaky little weasel, I would wager you would make a fine rogue, stealing comes naturally to you!&quot;<br /><br />Kaidog pretended not to hear her, unsheathing his rusty sword as if it were some glowing magical weapon of wonder, polishing it slowly and excruciatingly in front of her, nonchalant and pretending to be uninterested. &quot;Well, at least I don't try and memorize useless goobeldy-gook spells. &nbsp;What good are you in a fight? At least I am growing stronger with each training session, and soon my sword will be more feared in Norrath than any warrior who has ever lived!&quot;<br /><br />Karadana snorted, laughter capturing her with short stifled giggles that shook her tall frame. &nbsp;&quot;Warrior? HAH! You could not win a fight against a blind wolf pup, what makes you think anyone will ever fear you?&quot;<br /><br />They would go on like this for hours, their words like barbed arrows flying through the air. &nbsp;As fate would have it, their families lived in adjacent huts in Halas, and they would walk to school every morning, at first out of coincidence, eventually out of habit, and finally, in years to come, out of blossoming affection and blooming attraction towards one another.<br /><br />Kaidog chuckled to himself as he began to collect the scattered contents of the tacklebox, his mind landing on a particular incident that had occurred only weeks before Karadana had embarked on her journey south, and with a stabbing cringe of regret, or perhaps lonliness, he allowed the memory to wash over him, like a bittersweet blanket of momentary comfort.<br /><br />It was a day many years ago, ten or more perhaps, and he and Karadana had been sent south to Qeynos Hills in search of components for her father's alchemy. &nbsp;Fearing to send her alone, Karad had asked Tundra Jack to send his son Kaidog along as protection for the young shamaness, and agreeing, Kaidog and Karadana had set off through the snowy tunnels of Everfrost peaks, only 16 years old but full of so much life and enthusiasm that they had barely slept the night before in anticipation of their journey. &nbsp;<br /><br />Arriving in Qeynos after the light had fallen from the sky, Kaidog and Karadana had set up camp along the banks of a hot spring in Qeynos, nestled between the hilly valleys of the expansive plains, out of sight enough to be safe but not enough to be unaware. &nbsp;Karadana and Kaidog had spent many nights out on the tundra together as children, with their families in tow, racing across frozen stretches of glacier chasing snow bunnies and penguins, squealing and shouting. &nbsp;For some reason, tonight was different. &nbsp;There was no clucking mother to tell them to bathe, or go to bed, or eat their stew. &nbsp;There was no father to tell them to gather the firewood or set up the tents. &nbsp;They were grown-ups now, alone, responsible for themselves, and it was a taste of freedom that served only to whet the appetite of them both, making them long more for the adventures and independence that they were merely scratching the surface of now, tonight. &nbsp;Karadana unabashedly stripped off her skins and boots, tossing them carelessly into a pile alongside the tent.<br /><br />He had seen her naked a thousand times in the many years they had known one another, but for some reason, he was just now beginning to notice that girl parts and boy parts were very different. &nbsp;<br /><br />He tried not to watch her as she glided towards the water, her hair long and braided down her back, the curves of her hip and buttocks glistening with sweat in the moonlight as she walked into the waist-deep water, a sigh of contentment escaping her lips as the water washed across her weary muscles.<br /><br />&quot;Let's go, you great stinking bear. I don't want to smell you all day tomorrow, get your hairy ass into this water and rinse off, for the love of Rodcet.&quot; &nbsp;She began to unfurl her braid over her shoulder, the water soaking the tip of it as she slowly unwound the golden plait of hair, her hands running through it patiently to relieve it of the knots and tangles of sweat and dirt that had accumulated as they had journeyed.<br /><br />Kaidog stood up, swooning slightly, the blood flowing downward now and away from his brain, and suddenly shy, and he tossed a carrot towards her in the water. &quot;Here woman, make me a stew, it's about all you can do with those crappy ingredients you carry around with you everywhere.&quot; &nbsp;He slowly took off his coat, stripped quickly before he lost his courage, and ran into the water, splashing her thoroughly as he stormed down the banks, her squeals of indignation echoing across the water and bouncing off the red stone cliffs above them.<br /><br />&quot;QUIET woman, do you want to wake the gnolls? That would be just what I need, you bringing down all of Blackburrow onto our heads, and I would have to of course save you again, because you are as helpless as a kitten with your silly spellbook and stinky vials of potion.&quot;<br /><br />He watched her as she ignored him, her hands now cupping the water up towards her face, scrubbing the dirt gently off of her rosy cheeks, her hair now completely loose and golden and soaked in water. &nbsp;Her hair was always something he loved about her, but right now, he was thinking about the other parts, the parts below the water he couldn't help but notice as she casually bathed herself, arms working efficiently to stretch and reach behind her shoulders and down her thighs. &nbsp;How had he missed this transformation? She was no longer the flat-chested skinny girl he had loved to hate, she was now a tall goddess, glistening woman-flesh and curves, eyes as blue as sapphire, freckles across her nose and shoulders, her breasts hidden by her hair but nonetheless full and round, skin soft as goose down, legs long and graceful....GOD, what was he thinknig? This was Karadana, who used to pick her nose in class, Karadana who would throw rocks at his head during the village bonfires, Karadana who...who....who he thought he may love. LOVE? Did he just THINK that word? Love?<br /><br />As if not in control of his own body, he sunk lower into the water, his body betraying him now as he yearned for her, and boldly he swam towards her in the water and crouched low, the tip of his nose barely inches from her face. &nbsp;Her eyelashes were dark and thick and beaded with water, and the warmth of her breath assaulted his face as she questioned him.<br /><br />&quot;What in the name of all that is good and holy are you doing?&quot;<br /><br />&quot;Karadana....&quot; he whispered, his eyes closing, his lips reaching towards hers.<br /><br />With an echoing crack, her hand met his face in an open-handed slap, jolting him from his momentary lapse in sanity, chasing the blood from his loins and back into his burning cheeks. &nbsp;He didn't know which hurt worse, the sting of embarassment or the slap of her fierce little hand on his jaw.<br /><br />&quot;I don't know what on earth THAT was about, but I suggest we build a fire, eat dinner, and go to bed. &nbsp;I am going to pretend like that did not just happen, you lumbering oaf.&quot;<br /><br />Kaidog stormed out of the water, grabbed his skins, and donned them hastily. &nbsp;He looked her way, and he thought he saw a smile dance across her lips as she turned to face him, naked in the moonlight, slowly dressing, too slowly, he thought. &nbsp;<br /><br />Night fell, and as he drifted off into restless sleep, he felt the warm press of her body against his, beneath the same large animal skin, and he was sure it was merely for warmth, although the night was balmy and mild. &nbsp;As her head pressed against his shoulder and she drifted into her comatic sleep, he placed his large hand over her small one, and squeezed gently.<br /><br />He wasn't sure, but with a knowing smile, he thought he felt her squeeze back.
						</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td valign="bottom" class="smalltext">
							<table width="100%" border="0"><tr>
								<td align="left" colspan="2" class="smalltext">
								</td>
							</tr><tr>
								<td align="left" valign="bottom" class="smalltext">
								</td>
								<td align="right" valign="bottom" class="smalltext">
									<img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/ip.gif" alt="" border="0" /> Logged
								</td>
							</tr></table>
						</td>
					</tr>
				</table>
			</td></tr>
		</table>
	</td></tr>
</table>
